      "worked": "The line $6x-y-2=0$ has gradient $6$, so the perpendicular tangent gradient is $-\\dfrac16$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}y'&=\\dfrac{(x-2)-(x+4)}{(x-2)^2}\\\\y'&=-\\dfrac{6}{(x-2)^2}\\\\-\\dfrac{6}{(x-2)^2}&=-\\dfrac16\\\\(x-2)^2&=36\\\\x&=-4\\text{ or }8.\\end{align*}$$\n\nAt $x=-4$, $y=0$; at $x=8$, $y=2$. Using point–gradient form,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}y-0&=-\\dfrac16(x+4)\\\\y&=-\\dfrac{x}{6}-\\dfrac23,\\\\y-2&=-\\dfrac16(x-8)\\\\y&=-\\dfrac{x}{6}+\\dfrac{10}{3}.\\end{align*}$$",
